1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,682
If it feels comfortable, settle into a position that asks little of you.

2
00:00:05,632 --> 00:00:09,044
Let your breathing find its own pace.

3
00:00:09,044 --> 00:00:11,985
Your eyes may remain open.

4
00:00:11,985 --> 00:00:15,382
There is nothing to solve or memorize.

5
00:00:15,382 --> 00:00:25,206
This is an imagined journey through Olympic National Park, guided by what its landscapes and the people who study and care for them can teach us.

6
00:00:25,206 --> 00:00:28,794
I begin with the outline of a peninsula.

7
00:00:28,794 --> 00:00:35,926
Mountains occupy the interior; a narrow part of the park reaches along the Pacific shore.

8
00:00:35,926 --> 00:00:44,000
Around these protected places are Tribal lands, towns, working forests and other public lands.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,471
I picture those boundaries lightly, because water and wildlife continue across them.

10
00:00:50,471 --> 00:00:54,279
Human relationships reach across them too.

11
00:00:54,279 --> 00:01:01,147
My attention will travel from rock and ice to a lake, a meadow, a forest and a river.

12
00:01:01,147 --> 00:01:05,309
The journey changes scale as it changes place.

13
00:01:05,309 --> 00:01:08,779
It is not a continuous walking route.

14
00:01:08,779 --> 00:01:15,471
I can move between these settings in imagination, allowing their differences to become clear.

15
00:01:15,471 --> 00:01:21,000
Before entering them, I make room for the people whose homelands they are.

16
00:01:23,172 --> 00:01:40,063
I hold the peninsula in view while listening to the names of eight contemporary Tribes with longstanding connections here: the Lower Elwha Klallam, Jamestown S’Klallam, Port Gamble S’Klallam, Skokomish, Quinault, Hoh, Quileute and Makah.

17
00:01:40,013 --> 00:01:44,881
These names belong to distinct communities and governments.

18
00:01:44,881 --> 00:01:49,146
They are not different names for a single shared account.

19
00:01:49,146 --> 00:01:56,278
The landscape I am imagining has a human history that did not begin with a national park boundary.

20
00:01:56,278 --> 00:02:04,454
A river can be a fishery, a place of family connection, a travel corridor and a focus of present-day work.

21
00:02:04,454 --> 00:02:09,293
Those meanings cannot be read from the shape of its banks alone.

22
00:02:09,293 --> 00:02:15,763
I do not imagine speaking for these communities or entering knowledge they have not offered.

23
00:02:15,763 --> 00:02:24,704
I can instead attend to their own accounts, with the understanding that a scenic view is only one way of encountering a place.

24
00:02:24,704 --> 00:02:29,984
Recognizing living homelands changes the question of protection.

25
00:02:29,984 --> 00:02:37,028
It brings the people making decisions into the present, alongside the habitats being cared for.

26
00:02:39,188 --> 00:02:43,844
I imagine the lines of a map meeting the movement of a bird.

27
00:02:43,794 --> 00:02:51,882
The bird crosses water, turns toward an offshore rock, and passes through boundaries it does not perceive.

28
00:02:51,882 --> 00:02:56,250
Its habitat can involve more than one institution.

29
00:02:56,250 --> 00:03:11,059
Along Olympic’s coast, intertidal areas also lie within the Olympic Coast National Marine Sanctuary, while offshore islands belong to the Washington Maritime National Wildlife Refuge Complex.

30
00:03:11,059 --> 00:03:19,853
The park’s memorandum of understanding with eight peninsula Tribes provides a framework for government-to-government coordination.

31
00:03:19,853 --> 00:03:25,279
Each government retains its own responsibilities and perspective.

32
00:03:25,279 --> 00:03:32,279
A connected landscape calls for relationships among those responsible for different parts of it.

33
00:03:32,279 --> 00:03:37,426
I let the movement of the imagined bird make that connection tangible.

34
00:03:37,426 --> 00:03:44,250
A nesting place and a feeding place may be separate on a map while remaining parts of one life.

35
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:47,514
Stewardship can attend to both.

36
00:03:47,514 --> 00:03:55,162
It can also extend beyond habitat into the care of objects, records and cultural places.

37
00:03:55,162 --> 00:03:59,838
At Ozette, that different kind of care comes into focus.

38
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:07,014
I turn my attention toward the Makah Cultural and Research Center.

39
00:04:06,964 --> 00:04:17,258
Its museum interprets materials from the Ozette village archaeological site, connecting objects with Makah life through a Makah institution.

40
00:04:17,258 --> 00:04:26,744
I imagine looking closely at the material and workmanship of an object, while remembering that a shape alone cannot tell its whole story.

41
00:04:26,744 --> 00:04:34,273
A preserved object, an oral account and an exposed layer of rock are different kinds of evidence.

42
00:04:34,273 --> 00:04:39,376
Each can answer some questions and leave others beyond its reach.

43
00:04:39,376 --> 00:04:43,861
Here, I follow the museum’s own interpretation.

44
00:04:43,861 --> 00:04:53,155
I do not picture an archaeological place as a world without living descendants, or supply a story where I have not been given one.

45
00:04:53,155 --> 00:04:57,523
There is room to notice both continuity and change.

46
00:04:57,523 --> 00:05:02,273
Something can survive while the circumstances around it alter.

47
00:05:02,273 --> 00:05:07,714
That thought stays with me as my attention moves into a much older record.

48
00:05:07,714 --> 00:05:13,905
The rocks of the Olympic Mountains carry a history whose beginning was beneath the sea.

49
00:05:16,068 --> 00:05:20,327
I picture a simple model of the edge of a continent.

50
00:05:20,277 --> 00:05:26,644
This is an imagined explanation, not a view I could watch unfold during a visit.

51
00:05:26,644 --> 00:05:33,894
Offshore, the Juan de Fuca plate moves beneath North America at the Cascadia subduction zone.

52
00:05:33,894 --> 00:05:43,453
Material at that converging margin can be scraped, compressed and gathered into what geologists call an accretionary complex.

53
00:05:43,453 --> 00:05:49,909
Olympic’s basalt and sedimentary rocks include material with offshore origins.

54
00:05:49,909 --> 00:05:55,909
Uplift, folding and erosion helped turn that material into the mountains.

55
00:05:55,909 --> 00:06:07,424
I allow the sequence to unfold slowly in my attention: rock forms, rock is moved and deformed, and water and ice remove parts of the rising landscape.

56
00:06:07,424 --> 00:06:17,615
Basalt can record volcanic activity on an ancient seafloor without making the Olympic range a chain of volcanoes like the nearby Cascades.

57
00:06:17,615 --> 00:06:23,277
The origin of a rock and the building of a mountain are different parts of the account.

58
00:06:23,277 --> 00:06:30,218
Once the land stands high, another kind of movement begins to leave its shape in the valleys.

59
00:06:32,396 --> 00:06:36,184
I imagine arriving beside Lake Crescent.

60
00:06:36,134 --> 00:06:41,384
Mountains and clouds are reflected across a surface that seems still.

61
00:06:41,384 --> 00:06:45,987
Beneath that stillness is a basin shaped by moving ice.

62
00:06:45,987 --> 00:06:53,355
Glaciers eroded and carried rock; after the ice withdrew, the basin remained to hold water.

63
00:06:53,355 --> 00:06:59,870
Across Olympic, mountain cirques and glacial valleys preserve related traces.

64
00:06:59,870 --> 00:07:06,075
Mountain glaciers and the continental ice sheet followed different paths and histories.

65
00:07:06,075 --> 00:07:11,208
Lake Crescent’s connections changed again after glaciation.

66
00:07:11,208 --> 00:07:20,120
The National Park Service describes a massive landslide about seven thousand years ago that separated it from Lake Sutherland.

67
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:27,575
Isolation helped produce the distinctive Beardslee and Crescenti trout populations of Lake Crescent.

68
00:07:27,575 --> 00:07:33,090
I look from the outline of the basin to the idea of a fish moving within it.

69
00:07:33,090 --> 00:07:38,031
Geology has become part of a biological explanation.

70
00:07:38,031 --> 00:07:45,958
The shape of a place and its connections can influence the opportunities available to life over generations.

71
00:07:45,958 --> 00:07:53,531
Above the lake, the mountains influence another moving element: the weather arriving from the Pacific.

72
00:07:55,708 --> 00:08:00,761
I follow an imagined body of moist air inland from the ocean.

73
00:08:00,711 --> 00:08:07,020
As it rises over the mountain barrier, it cools and releases precipitation.

74
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:11,137
Western slopes intercept much of that moisture.

75
00:08:11,137 --> 00:08:18,534
Farther northeast, the peninsula lies in a rain shadow, with much drier regional conditions.

76
00:08:18,534 --> 00:08:25,299
This pattern helps explain how markedly different environments can belong to one peninsula.

77
00:08:25,299 --> 00:08:29,887
It does not predict the weather of a particular afternoon.

78
00:08:29,887 --> 00:08:39,299
The direction a slope faces, its elevation and the course of an individual storm can all add differences to the broader pattern.

79
00:08:39,299 --> 00:08:44,167
I picture water taking several paths through this landscape.

80
00:08:44,167 --> 00:08:47,623
Some arrives as rain in a low valley.

81
00:08:47,623 --> 00:08:50,843
Some remains in seasonal snow.

82
00:08:50,843 --> 00:08:55,696
Some becomes part of a glacier, stored as ice for longer.

83
00:08:55,696 --> 00:09:02,682
The mountain’s shape influences where the water goes, and the water helps shape what can live there.

84
00:09:02,682 --> 00:09:11,182
Holding one snowy part of that picture in view, I begin to notice a balance between what arrives and what is lost.

85
00:09:13,356 --> 00:09:19,806
A glacier gains mass through snowfall and loses it through melting and other processes.

86
00:09:19,756 --> 00:09:24,565
I imagine these as movements into and out of a store of ice.

87
00:09:24,565 --> 00:09:29,830
When losses repeatedly exceed gains, that store diminishes.

88
00:09:29,830 --> 00:09:35,903
The end of a glacier can retreat, while the body of the ice also becomes thinner.

89
00:09:35,903 --> 00:09:40,271
A single outline cannot describe all of the change.

90
00:09:40,271 --> 00:09:46,977
The Blue Glacier photographs in this course compare eighteen ninety-nine with two thousand eight.

91
00:09:46,977 --> 00:09:51,153
They are a historical pair, not a current view.

92
00:09:51,153 --> 00:09:58,168
Repeated measurements provide a longer account of ice storage than either image can offer alone.

93
00:09:58,168 --> 00:10:01,256
I follow the connection downhill.

94
00:10:01,256 --> 00:10:09,815
Ice contributes meltwater during the relatively dry summer, but different streams receive different mixtures of water.

95
00:10:09,815 --> 00:10:17,006
The broad evidence of ice loss does not give a precise future date or effect for every watershed.

96
00:10:17,006 --> 00:10:20,624
That local question will remain with us.

97
00:10:20,624 --> 00:10:27,888
For now, I let the snowy slopes open into the meadows and forest patches of Hurricane Ridge.

98
00:10:30,068 --> 00:10:38,812
In my imagined view from Hurricane Ridge, open meadows lie beside stands of trees, with mountain ridges beyond.

99
00:10:38,762 --> 00:10:43,086
Wildflowers bring my attention close to the ground.

100
00:10:43,086 --> 00:10:47,365
Black-tailed deer may graze in this high-country setting.

101
00:10:47,365 --> 00:10:55,394
A meadow and a sheltered group of trees occupy different growing conditions, even when they appear close together.

102
00:10:55,394 --> 00:10:58,380
Height influences temperature.

103
00:10:58,380 --> 00:11:05,409
The direction a slope faces changes the sunlight it receives and how long snow can persist.

104
00:11:05,409 --> 00:11:12,762
These differences help determine where plants establish and how much of each year is available for growth.

105
00:11:12,762 --> 00:11:20,703
The boundary between forest and open ground is a patchwork, rather than one perfect line around the mountain.

106
00:11:20,703 --> 00:11:24,542
I allow the season to become part of the scene.

107
00:11:24,542 --> 00:11:33,394
The same region that holds wet western forests also holds places where snow and a short growing period organize life.

108
00:11:33,394 --> 00:11:42,394
Looking from plants toward an animal of these meadows, I notice how much can depend on the opportunities of a brief mountain summer.

109
00:11:44,572 --> 00:11:49,125
I picture an Olympic marmot near the entrance to a burrow.

110
00:11:49,075 --> 00:11:54,854
This species occurs naturally in the Olympic Mountains and nowhere else.

111
00:11:54,854 --> 00:11:59,384
That restricted natural range is what endemic means.

112
00:11:59,384 --> 00:12:06,310
The burrow offers shelter, and a long hibernation carries the animal through much of the year.

113
00:12:06,310 --> 00:12:12,398
Its active season provides time to feed, reproduce and build reserves.

114
00:12:12,398 --> 00:12:17,884
A single imagined encounter is only a small window into that life.

115
00:12:17,884 --> 00:12:26,222
Olympic marmots mature relatively slowly for rodents, so lost breeding adults can take time to replace.

116
00:12:26,222 --> 00:12:32,766
The park has identified concerns about predation and changing habitat conditions.

117
00:12:32,766 --> 00:12:40,237
Repeated surveys across meadow patches can show where marmots remain, disappear or return.

118
00:12:40,237 --> 00:12:48,693
I hold those two scales together: one animal in a meadow, and a species distributed among mountain habitats.

119
00:12:48,693 --> 00:12:54,678
The first makes the life tangible; the second asks for repeated evidence.

120
00:12:54,678 --> 00:12:57,854
We will return to that distinction.

121
00:12:57,854 --> 00:13:05,825
Leaving the short mountain summer in imagination, I descend toward the very different layers of the Hoh rainforest.

122
00:13:08,004 --> 00:13:12,101
My attention moves upward through a green canopy.

123
00:13:12,051 --> 00:13:21,139
In the Hoh, bigleaf maples carry mosses and ferns along their branches, while conifers rise above the shaded ground.

124
00:13:21,139 --> 00:13:26,801
The smaller plants make trunks and limbs into additional living surfaces.

125
00:13:26,801 --> 00:13:30,919
An epiphyte is a plant growing on another plant.

126
00:13:30,919 --> 00:13:38,169
That description concerns its position; it does not by itself mean the plant is a parasite.

127
00:13:38,169 --> 00:13:45,786
I picture light reaching some leaves directly and passing through other leaves before it reaches the forest floor.

128
00:13:45,786 --> 00:13:52,301
A fallen tree can create an opening, changing the light available beneath the canopy.

129
00:13:52,301 --> 00:13:58,022
The forest has many layers and small habitats within the larger valley.

130
00:13:58,022 --> 00:14:04,095
The Hoh River connects this place with the Mount Olympus high country and the Pacific.

131
00:14:04,095 --> 00:14:12,272
Hoh also belongs beside Queets, Bogachiel and Quinault among Olympic’s western rainforest valleys.

132
00:14:12,272 --> 00:14:16,360
Their shared climate does not make them identical.

133
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:25,022
I carry the idea of these living layers into the Queets, where the remains of a tree draw my attention closer to the ground.

134
00:14:27,188 --> 00:14:33,873
I imagine standing among large Sitka spruce and moss-draped maples in the Queets valley.

135
00:14:33,823 --> 00:14:40,720
Elk browse in this wider forest setting, helping shape vegetation beneath the canopy.

136
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:47,809
Around the living trunks, the remains of earlier generations are still part of the habitat.

137
00:14:47,809 --> 00:14:53,735
Beside a fallen tree, I picture seedlings establishing on decaying wood.

138
00:14:53,735 --> 00:14:58,191
A trunk supporting this new growth is called a nurse log.

139
00:14:58,191 --> 00:15:03,412
Young roots can extend down into the soil as the tree develops.

140
00:15:03,412 --> 00:15:11,176
When the old wood eventually disappears, a line of trees and raised roots may preserve its former position.

141
00:15:11,176 --> 00:15:15,779
Fungi and invertebrates also use decomposing wood.

142
00:15:15,779 --> 00:15:19,544
Growth, feeding and decay occur together.

143
00:15:19,544 --> 00:15:27,662
I can follow a connection between generations without imagining that every fallen trunk develops in the same way.

144
00:15:27,662 --> 00:15:32,514
The forest’s history remains partly present in its structure.

145
00:15:32,514 --> 00:15:42,706
Wood can record that history through decay and new roots; fire leaves another kind of record, especially across the drier side of the peninsula.

146
00:15:44,860 --> 00:15:51,384
I let the imagined forest change from a wetter western setting toward a drier eastern one.

147
00:15:51,334 --> 00:15:56,481
Olympic’s fire histories differ across climate and exposure.

148
00:15:56,481 --> 00:16:03,525
Eastern forests have generally experienced fire more frequently than the wetter western forests.

149
00:16:03,525 --> 00:16:09,539
Tree rings, scars and charcoal can preserve evidence of earlier events.

150
00:16:09,539 --> 00:16:16,995
A forest standing today is one part of a longer sequence of disturbance, growth and recovery.

151
00:16:16,995 --> 00:16:19,966
A wet place can still burn.

152
00:16:19,966 --> 00:16:25,598
A historical interval between fires is not a schedule for the next one.

153
00:16:25,598 --> 00:16:32,451
Weather, fuels and the surrounding landscape influence what happens in a particular event.

154
00:16:32,451 --> 00:16:36,539
I do not need to turn this scene into a prediction.

155
00:16:36,539 --> 00:16:46,686
I can notice that the same green appearance may conceal different histories, and that an explanation needs more than one photograph or one visit.

156
00:16:46,686 --> 00:16:56,878
The differences between western and eastern Olympic become more tangible as my attention reaches Staircase, in the southeastern corner of the park.

157
00:16:59,052 --> 00:17:04,208
Here I picture Douglas-firs around the North Fork Skokomish River.

158
00:17:04,158 --> 00:17:11,143
Staircase offers a different forest setting from the moss-heavy valleys of the western peninsula.

159
00:17:11,143 --> 00:17:16,570
Moisture, exposure and elevation help shape the contrast.

160
00:17:16,570 --> 00:17:21,628
A broad view of Olympic needs both settings to remain distinct.

161
00:17:21,628 --> 00:17:30,040
I follow the river in imagination toward higher valleys and lakes, then return to the water passing through the forest.

162
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:34,026
Its present channel is only part of its story.

163
00:17:34,026 --> 00:17:36,673
Water levels change.

164
00:17:36,673 --> 00:17:39,393
Floods move material.

165
00:17:39,393 --> 00:17:43,673
Banks and vegetation interact with that movement.

166
00:17:43,673 --> 00:17:53,026
Rain, snowmelt and water delayed or stored within the watershed contribute in different proportions among seasons and rivers.

167
00:17:53,026 --> 00:17:58,320
The river becomes a corridor of processes rather than a fixed line.

168
00:17:58,320 --> 00:18:03,834
I can keep that wider idea while looking closely at a particular reach.

169
00:18:03,834 --> 00:18:16,834
Across the peninsula in Sol Duc, the shape of moving water becomes especially clear where it drops over a rocky ledge, then continues among the forest and its changing habitats.

170
00:18:19,004 --> 00:18:23,866
I imagine the water at Sol Duc Falls dropping over rock.

171
00:18:23,816 --> 00:18:32,036
The surrounding landscape connects old-growth forest, waterfalls, subalpine lakes and snowy peaks.

172
00:18:32,036 --> 00:18:40,301
Farther along the river corridor, pools, faster channels and quieter margins create different conditions.

173
00:18:40,301 --> 00:18:44,389
The river has a structure as well as a direction.

174
00:18:44,389 --> 00:18:51,228
Large wood elsewhere in a channel can redirect flow and help form pools and side channels.

175
00:18:51,228 --> 00:18:56,757
Shade and connections within the watershed also influence habitat.

176
00:18:56,757 --> 00:19:00,904
These relationships vary from reach to reach.

177
00:19:00,904 --> 00:19:06,683
Looking at one waterfall cannot tell me what every part of a river provides.

178
00:19:06,683 --> 00:19:14,757
At Sol Duc’s Salmon Cascades, migrating coho can be seen negotiating a natural step in the river.

179
00:19:14,757 --> 00:19:21,242
I picture the contrast between resting water and the movement needed to pass a cascade.

180
00:19:21,242 --> 00:19:26,595
The significance of each place depends partly on the fish using it.

181
00:19:26,595 --> 00:19:37,375
To understand that relationship, my attention follows a life that needs several habitats, at different stages, before returning to freshwater.

182
00:19:39,556 --> 00:19:43,932
I begin with eggs developing in freshwater gravel.

183
00:19:43,882 --> 00:19:49,118
Young salmon use rearing habitats before moving toward the sea.

184
00:19:49,118 --> 00:19:56,721
Anadromous describes fish that spend part of their lives at sea and return to freshwater to reproduce.

185
00:19:56,721 --> 00:20:01,471
Species and populations differ in their timing and needs.

186
00:20:01,471 --> 00:20:09,088
Sol Duc coho and Quinault sockeye belong to this wider account without following identical schedules.

187
00:20:09,088 --> 00:20:15,838
The imagined route passes through a sheltered rearing place, an estuary and the ocean.

188
00:20:15,838 --> 00:20:20,441
Each setting can matter for a different part of the life cycle.

189
00:20:20,441 --> 00:20:25,500
A habitat used for a short time may still be consequential.

190
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:30,397
The boundaries of the park contain only part of this moving life.

191
00:20:30,397 --> 00:20:34,618
I let the route turn back inland with an adult fish.

192
00:20:34,618 --> 00:20:41,853
An abundant return on one day does not describe every condition affecting a population’s future.

193
00:20:41,853 --> 00:20:48,191
Yet the return itself carries something beyond the possibility of another generation.

194
00:20:48,191 --> 00:20:54,765
Material acquired in the ocean is now moving upstream into freshwater communities.

195
00:20:56,940 --> 00:21:01,816
I follow that returning fish as a connection between places.

196
00:21:01,766 --> 00:21:05,899
Salmon bring marine-derived nutrients inland.

197
00:21:05,899 --> 00:21:13,781
Predators and scavengers feed on fish, and decomposition provides other routes into a food web.

198
00:21:13,781 --> 00:21:20,414
In the Queets valley, these relationships connect river, wildlife and forest.

199
00:21:20,414 --> 00:21:28,884
Eagles, otters and smaller organisms take part in movements of material that extend beyond the original fish.

200
00:21:28,884 --> 00:21:34,884
The idea joins the elk browsing and nurse-log decay we encountered earlier.

201
00:21:34,884 --> 00:21:40,516
A community continually rearranges living and once-living material.

202
00:21:40,516 --> 00:21:46,296
Water carries nutrients downstream; fish can carry them upstream.

203
00:21:46,296 --> 00:21:53,869
The forest and the ocean remain distinct habitats while participating in some of the same relationships.

204
00:21:53,869 --> 00:21:57,208
The quantities and pathways vary.

205
00:21:57,208 --> 00:22:05,237
I treat this imagined model as a way of understanding a connection, not as a measurement of every tree or stream.

206
00:22:05,237 --> 00:22:12,090
Within this broader pattern, particular populations still need particular places.

207
00:22:12,090 --> 00:22:22,369
My attention turns toward Lake Quinault, where a lake-dependent salmon story belongs alongside the Quinault Indian Nation’s own fisheries work.

208
00:22:24,548 --> 00:22:28,998
I picture Lake Quinault as part of a living watershed.

209
00:22:28,948 --> 00:22:38,933
Quinault blueback are a sockeye salmon population with deep cultural, ecological and economic importance to the Quinault Indian Nation.

210
00:22:38,933 --> 00:22:45,624
Their life cycle is tied to this lake and river system, as well as to the ocean beyond it.

211
00:22:45,624 --> 00:22:53,036
Freshwater residence gives the lake a role that a generic migration outline cannot fully describe.

212
00:22:53,036 --> 00:22:59,124
The Nation’s fisheries information follows these particular fish and their needs.

213
00:22:59,124 --> 00:23:03,051
I let that specificity remain in the picture.

214
00:23:03,051 --> 00:23:12,169
The word salmon brings together different life strategies; caring for populations requires attending to those differences.

215
00:23:12,169 --> 00:23:19,124
Lake Crescent earlier showed how connections and isolation can influence life in a basin.

216
00:23:19,124 --> 00:23:28,536
Here the question has a different setting and history, with the Nation’s assessments guiding an inquiry into habitat and the lake’s future.

217
00:23:28,536 --> 00:23:38,448
I keep those stories distinct as the journey moves toward the Elwha, where a major human intervention reopened a route that dams had obstructed.

218
00:23:40,612 --> 00:23:46,415
I imagine the Elwha corridor before and after its two dams were removed.

219
00:23:46,365 --> 00:23:54,586
Removal began in two thousand eleven; the removal of Glines Canyon Dam was completed in two thousand fourteen.

220
00:23:54,586 --> 00:23:59,336
The river reopened after roughly a century of obstruction.

221
00:23:59,336 --> 00:24:06,836
For the Lower Elwha Klallam Tribe, this work belongs within a much longer relationship with the river.

222
00:24:06,836 --> 00:24:13,247
Opening passage also released sediment and exposed former reservoir surfaces.

223
00:24:13,247 --> 00:24:19,453
Revegetation and floodplain restoration became parts of the continuing work.

224
00:24:19,453 --> 00:24:27,056
Fish, plants, floods and sediment respond over different timescales after a structure is gone.

225
00:24:27,056 --> 00:24:33,027
I allow the imagined before-and-after view to widen into that longer process.

226
00:24:33,027 --> 00:24:38,894
A major action can change the conditions under which an ecosystem develops.

227
00:24:38,894 --> 00:24:42,630
The response does not arrive all at once.

228
00:24:42,630 --> 00:24:51,291
A photograph can reveal a transformation in the landscape, while repeated observations address what happens within it.

229
00:24:51,291 --> 00:24:58,850
Following the newly opened river, I arrive at the question of how researchers know which fish have returned.

230
00:25:01,020 --> 00:25:08,985
I picture a researcher returning to a stretch of the Elwha, comparing observations with earlier surveys.

231
00:25:08,935 --> 00:25:14,788
Studies have examined fish distributions before and after dam removal.

232
00:25:14,788 --> 00:25:22,008
Another method samples environmental DNA, traces that organisms shed into the water.

233
00:25:22,008 --> 00:25:31,346
Species-specific tests found evidence that most of the targeted migratory fish species had passed both former dam sites.

234
00:25:31,346 --> 00:25:36,008
The timing and upstream extent differed among species.

235
00:25:36,008 --> 00:25:39,170
Each observation has a reach.

236
00:25:39,170 --> 00:25:45,832
Detecting a species in newly accessible water supports an account of use or passage.

237
00:25:45,832 --> 00:25:54,538
It does not, by itself, establish the number of fish, successful reproduction or long-term resilience.

238
00:25:54,538 --> 00:26:01,067
Several kinds of evidence can be brought together to investigate those different questions.

239
00:26:01,067 --> 00:26:07,935
I can recognize the importance of an opened route while leaving the fuller recovery question open.

240
00:26:07,935 --> 00:26:12,302
The river’s response also extends beyond fish.

241
00:26:12,302 --> 00:26:21,552
Material once held upstream has begun moving toward the mouth, where the freshwater corridor meets a changing coastal environment.

242
00:26:23,732 --> 00:26:28,138
I imagine following a grain of sediment downstream.

243
00:26:28,088 --> 00:26:32,235
A river transports material as well as water.

244
00:26:32,235 --> 00:26:38,176
At its mouth, that material enters a coast shaped by waves and currents.

245
00:26:38,176 --> 00:26:47,720
Sand, gravel and finer particles can be deposited or moved again, altering the surfaces available to living organisms.

246
00:26:47,720 --> 00:26:51,558
The Elwha provides a documented example.

247
00:26:51,558 --> 00:26:58,308
Dam removal released trapped sediment and changed coastal habitats near the river mouth.

248
00:26:58,308 --> 00:27:06,103
United States Geological Survey studies found different biological responses in different places.

249
00:27:06,103 --> 00:27:14,514
Some changes created habitat for soft-bottom organisms; the effects were not uniform across the coast.

250
00:27:14,514 --> 00:27:21,661
I let this particular event explain a process without making it stand for the whole Pacific shore.

251
00:27:21,661 --> 00:27:28,838
Beyond river mouths, Olympic’s beaches alternate with rocky headlands and offshore stacks.

252
00:27:28,838 --> 00:27:35,206
The scale of my attention shifts from transported grains to larger forms of rock.

253
00:27:35,206 --> 00:27:43,323
Water continues to act upon those forms, while life finds opportunities in their temporary arrangements.

254
00:27:45,484 --> 00:27:51,963
I imagine looking through the eroded opening of Hole-in-the-Wall, north of Rialto Beach.

255
00:27:51,913 --> 00:28:00,016
The Pacific lies beyond the arch. Headlands, beaches and offshore stacks give the shore a varied outline.

256
00:28:00,016 --> 00:28:06,340
Differences in rock and exposure influence how erosion shapes this edge of the land.

257
00:28:06,340 --> 00:28:10,031
The shapes also offer places to live.

258
00:28:10,031 --> 00:28:18,163
Offshore islands provide nesting habitat for seabirds, including common murres and tufted puffins.

259
00:28:18,163 --> 00:28:22,619
Sheltered basins retain water as the tide falls.

260
00:28:22,619 --> 00:28:29,016
A rock form belongs both to a geological account and to the lives using it now.

261
00:28:29,016 --> 00:28:37,972
The park’s coastline participates in a larger marine system, with feeding waters and protected islands beyond the beach.

262
00:28:37,972 --> 00:28:43,575
I hold that wider relationship while looking down toward one small basin.

263
00:28:43,575 --> 00:28:53,987
The distant mountains and a tidepool differ greatly in size, yet both ask me to notice how physical conditions create opportunities for life.

264
00:28:53,987 --> 00:28:59,708
At the waterline, the timing of exposure becomes especially important.

265
00:29:01,884 --> 00:29:05,893
I picture water withdrawing from a rocky shore.

266
00:29:05,843 --> 00:29:10,799
Some surfaces remain underwater while others enter the air.

267
00:29:10,799 --> 00:29:18,343
Height, wave exposure and the shape of the shore influence the conditions organisms experience.

268
00:29:18,343 --> 00:29:25,019
A tidepool that retains water differs from a neighboring surface left directly exposed.

269
00:29:25,019 --> 00:29:32,005
Barnacles, mussels, seaweeds and other organisms occupy this changing boundary.

270
00:29:32,005 --> 00:29:40,284
Small differences in position can bring substantial differences in time underwater, temperature or exposure.

271
00:29:40,284 --> 00:29:48,093
The pool I am imagining is one moment within a repeating cycle, rather than a complete account of the place.

272
00:29:48,093 --> 00:29:55,740
National Park Service monitoring returns to intertidal communities and measures conditions through time.

273
00:29:55,740 --> 00:30:02,181
Repeated observations help distinguish an unusual day from a lasting change.

274
00:30:02,181 --> 00:30:11,063
I can stay briefly with the small world of the pool, then widen attention to the people returning with observations and records.

275
00:30:11,063 --> 00:30:21,696
Their work connects this coastal scene with a larger question running through the entire journey: what can the evidence tell us, and where does it leave us still learning?

276
00:30:23,876 --> 00:30:33,047
I bring the different places back into view: a changing glacier, a meadow, a forest, a river and a pool beside the sea.

277
00:30:32,997 --> 00:30:37,688
Monitoring follows selected indicators over time.

278
00:30:37,688 --> 00:30:45,129
Focused research investigates mechanisms and questions that those records alone may not answer.

279
00:30:45,129 --> 00:30:51,129
A baseline can make change visible without automatically explaining its cause.

280
00:30:51,129 --> 00:30:55,702
Three distinctions help me continue gently and carefully.

281
00:30:55,702 --> 00:30:59,879
An observed trend differs from its explanation.

282
00:30:59,879 --> 00:31:04,850
A result from one place or interval may not apply everywhere.

283
00:31:04,850 --> 00:31:11,041
And an unresolved question differs from something this journey simply has not covered.

284
00:31:11,041 --> 00:31:18,555
The final six places in the spiral are questions, grounded in the evidence already introduced.

285
00:31:18,555 --> 00:31:26,261
They are investigations framed for this course, not an official ranking of the park’s research priorities.

286
00:31:26,261 --> 00:31:29,408
I do not need to answer them now.

287
00:31:29,408 --> 00:31:36,305
I can notice the known basis, the remaining uncertainty and a possible way to learn more.

288
00:31:36,305 --> 00:31:41,820
The first returns attention to water stored high in the mountains as ice.

289
00:31:43,996 --> 00:31:49,255
I picture the glacier again, then a stream flowing in late summer.

290
00:31:49,205 --> 00:31:56,014
Measurements document changes in ice storage, and meltwater contributes to summer flow.

291
00:31:56,014 --> 00:32:00,322
The stream also receives water from other sources.

292
00:32:00,322 --> 00:32:07,720
Snow, rain, groundwater and conditions within the channel can all matter to its response.

293
00:32:07,720 --> 00:32:17,514
The question is specific: how will declining glacier storage change late-summer flow and temperature in individual Olympic watersheds?

294
00:32:17,514 --> 00:32:22,205
The uncertainty concerns local timing and magnitude.

295
00:32:22,205 --> 00:32:26,675
It does not undo the evidence that ice has been lost.

296
00:32:26,675 --> 00:32:37,308
I imagine records being placed alongside one another: repeated glacier measurements, streamflow, water temperature and weather in the same basin.

297
00:32:37,308 --> 00:32:44,102
Comparing them could help test alternative explanations of the water arriving downstream.

298
00:32:44,102 --> 00:32:50,896
The answer would need to carry its uncertainty, rather than assign one date to the whole park.

299
00:32:50,896 --> 00:32:55,970
I leave that inquiry open, with a clearer sense of what would help.

300
00:32:55,970 --> 00:33:02,278
From water stored as ice, attention moves toward the conditions supporting plants.

301
00:33:04,452 --> 00:33:09,461
I return in imagination to a slope where trees meet a meadow.

302
00:33:09,411 --> 00:33:17,367
Regional research has reported changes in plant timing and tree establishment in subalpine settings.

303
00:33:17,367 --> 00:33:23,308
Baselines and focused studies help investigate what those observations mean.

304
00:33:23,308 --> 00:33:29,808
One seedling or one dry season cannot establish the whole direction of a community.

305
00:33:29,808 --> 00:33:40,440
I wonder where changes in moisture, snow duration and disturbance alter Olympic plant communities, and which processes explain the differences.

306
00:33:40,440 --> 00:33:45,528
A sheltered slope and an exposed one may respond differently.

307
00:33:45,528 --> 00:33:50,043
More than one influence may be acting at the same time.

308
00:33:50,043 --> 00:34:00,558
A possible investigation would revisit vegetation plots across contrasting slopes and compare those records with snow and weather observations.

309
00:34:00,558 --> 00:34:05,323
Competing explanations would remain part of the comparison.

310
00:34:05,323 --> 00:34:13,543
The regional evidence motivates the question without supplying a complete causal answer for every Olympic forest.

311
00:34:13,543 --> 00:34:19,264
I allow the meadow to remain a living place rather than a finished forecast.

312
00:34:19,264 --> 00:34:26,014
The conditions changing there also matter to the marmot whose range is held within these mountains.

313
00:34:28,196 --> 00:34:32,675
I picture a meadow where a survey has found no marmot.

314
00:34:32,625 --> 00:34:38,140
An empty observation is a beginning, not a complete explanation.

315
00:34:38,140 --> 00:34:46,581
Repeated visits help researchers distinguish a missed detection from a change in which habitat patches are occupied.

316
00:34:46,581 --> 00:34:53,081
Occupancy describes presence in a place rather than a direct count of every animal.

317
00:34:53,081 --> 00:34:57,420
The question asks what lies behind a missing colony.

318
00:34:57,420 --> 00:35:05,905
How much might be explained by habitat conditions, predation, movement among patches or imperfect detection?

319
00:35:05,905 --> 00:35:16,052
The park has identified concerns about predation and climate-related habitat conditions, but their contributions can differ among places.

320
00:35:16,052 --> 00:35:23,875
I imagine returning to the same meadow, and comparing those visits with other carefully studied patches.

321
00:35:23,875 --> 00:35:30,905
Occupancy models and permitted ecological studies could help evaluate the alternatives.

322
00:35:30,905 --> 00:35:35,552
One quiet hike would not become proof of a single cause.

323
00:35:35,552 --> 00:35:40,581
The distinction leaves room for both concern and careful learning.

324
00:35:40,581 --> 00:35:50,008
I carry it from the mountain meadow toward the restored river, where detecting an animal also differs from understanding its longer future.

325
00:35:52,180 --> 00:35:57,130
I follow the Elwha upstream beyond the former dam sites.

326
00:35:57,080 --> 00:36:06,962
Surveys and environmental DNA have documented renewed habitat use, with differences among species in timing and extent.

327
00:36:06,962 --> 00:36:12,139
Those observations establish an important change in access.

328
00:36:12,139 --> 00:36:16,874
They do not alone establish a self-sustaining population.

329
00:36:16,874 --> 00:36:26,389
The deeper question is which newly accessible habitats support reproduction and survival across generations of returning fish.

330
00:36:26,389 --> 00:36:34,418
Passage, reproduction and long-term population trends are related, but they are different things to measure.

331
00:36:34,418 --> 00:36:42,124
Conditions in the ocean can influence returns as well as conditions inside the restored river corridor.

332
00:36:42,124 --> 00:36:51,051
I imagine an inquiry bringing distribution surveys together with juvenile and adult monitoring and habitat measurements.

333
00:36:51,051 --> 00:36:56,874
That combination could connect places being used with outcomes over time.

334
00:36:56,874 --> 00:37:04,727
A successful passage would keep its significance while the larger recovery question remained under investigation.

335
00:37:04,727 --> 00:37:14,815
I let the river reach the coast again, where repeated observations also reveal communities changing under more than one possible influence.

336
00:37:16,980 --> 00:37:22,356
I return to the tidepool and imagine it observed through many seasons.

337
00:37:22,306 --> 00:37:27,115
Monitoring records community composition and temperature.

338
00:37:27,115 --> 00:37:35,365
Coastal chemistry measurements help investigate ocean acidification and the conditions organisms experience.

339
00:37:35,365 --> 00:37:40,983
A change in abundance can coincide with several environmental shifts.

340
00:37:40,983 --> 00:37:50,512
How can researchers separate natural variability from warming, acidification and local disturbance at Olympic intertidal sites?

341
00:37:50,512 --> 00:37:57,321
The question does not assume that every cause is equally likely, or that none has been studied.

342
00:37:57,321 --> 00:38:03,571
It asks how the evidence can distinguish their contributions in a particular setting.

343
00:38:03,571 --> 00:38:14,277
I picture biological surveys considered alongside temperature and chemistry records, with comparisons among sites that have different exposures.

344
00:38:14,277 --> 00:38:19,351
Suitable tests of mechanisms could strengthen an explanation.

345
00:38:19,351 --> 00:38:25,159
Monitoring may reveal a pattern before its causes are fully separated.

346
00:38:25,159 --> 00:38:28,762
That is a useful limit to recognize.

347
00:38:28,762 --> 00:38:39,909
One final inquiry brings this attention to evidence into the decisions made about a particular lake, with the people responsible for its care remaining central.

348
00:38:42,068 --> 00:38:48,915
I hold Lake Quinault in mind alongside the Quinault Indian Nation’s planning and assessments.

349
00:38:48,865 --> 00:39:00,836
That work identifies ecological concerns, climate resilience and information gaps, including questions about blueback habitat and nearshore conditions.

350
00:39:00,836 --> 00:39:05,012
The lake is not reduced to one solved mechanism.

351
00:39:05,012 --> 00:39:13,174
Which information would most improve decisions about habitat, water quality and resilience for blueback?

352
00:39:13,174 --> 00:39:21,600
A useful next inquiry would identify a particular gap, the decision it affects and an appropriate measurement.

353
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:26,350
The Nation’s own assessments and priorities guide that learning.

354
00:39:26,350 --> 00:39:32,233
More data does not automatically settle questions of values or governance.

355
00:39:32,233 --> 00:39:47,821
I leave the question open and allow the whole peninsula to return: Lake Crescent’s basin, Hurricane Ridge’s meadows, the Hoh and Queets forests, Staircase and Sol Duc, Quinault and Elwha, and the Pacific shore.

356
00:39:47,821 --> 00:39:53,394
Each place has kept its differences while becoming part of a connected account.

357
00:39:53,394 --> 00:39:59,321
If you wish, notice the support beneath you and the ordinary sounds around you.

358
00:39:59,321 --> 00:40:02,953
Let attention return at an easy pace.

359
00:40:02,953 --> 00:40:07,865
The journey can end here, with no need to hold every detail.

360
00:40:07,865 --> 00:40:17,586
I carry one simple practice from these landscapes: to look closely, follow a connection, and leave room for what remains to be learned.
